# Migration image (Flyway) — the entire Netty stack (netty-handler,
# netty-codec-http, netty-codec-http2, netty-codec-compression, ...) is
# shaded into Flyway's own core-io module (core-io-3.12.0.jar), not a
# vendor JDBC driver we ship or control. Not yet patched upstream by
# Flyway as of 13.5.0 (latest available) — every one of these is a
# server-side HTTP/2, SPDY, or compression-codec DoS in that embedded
# Netty copy.
#
# Accepted risk: this image only ever runs `flyway migrate` against our
# own private RDS instance from inside a locked-down VPC — it never
# accepts inbound traffic, so there is no attacker-reachable HTTP/2/SPDY
# server here for any of these to trigger against. Revisit and remove
# these lines once Flyway ships a patched core-io release (each new scan
# has been surfacing more CVE IDs from the same embedded Netty copy, so
# expect to append rather than assume this list is final).
CVE-2026-44249
CVE-2026-45416
CVE-2026-50010
CVE-2026-56745
CVE-2026-56819
CVE-2026-59901
CVE-2026-55831
CVE-2026-55833

# Vendor JDBC drivers we don't use (Databricks, SQL Server) — bundled inside
# the flyway/flyway image alongside the Postgres driver this project actually
# runs migrations with. Same reasoning as the Netty block above: this image
# only ever runs `flyway migrate` against our own private RDS instance from
# inside a locked-down VPC, so there is no attacker-reachable network service
# here for a client-side JDBC library CVE to be exploited through.
#
# flyway/drivers/databricks-jdbc-3.4.1.jar (jackson-core, jackson-databind,
# httpcore5, httpcore5-h2 bundled inside it):
